<p>Anyone who listens to podcasts in the car knows the move. The host talks, you turn it up for the guest, the host comes back shouting, you turn it down. After enough drives like that, one simple question was left: why doesn't the player do this itself?</p>
<p>Ordinary normalisation treats an episode as one unit. It sets its level, but it doesn't notice that two people inside it are talking at two different levels. LevelCast was built for exactly that gap.</p>

<h2>What it's built on</h2>
<ul class="checks">
  <li><strong>The processing happens on the phone.</strong> Levelling, intro detection and pace matching run on the device while the episode plays.</li>
  <li><strong>No account and no server in the middle.</strong> The audio comes straight from the producer's server, and whatever you save stays on your phone.</li>
  <li><strong>Producers' content stays theirs.</strong> No copy, no proxy, and downloads are counted on their side. <a href="{{ collections.byKey.producers[lang] }}">More for producers</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbers only when we measured them.</strong> The figures on this site come from measuring a real minute, <a href="{{ collections.byKey.listen[lang] }}">and you can listen to it</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who builds it</h2>
<p>LevelCast is built in Israel by Amit Sagy, for iPhone, in Hebrew and English. It's coming soon to the App Store.</p>
